Parkinson's Disease Prediction 🧠🎤

This repository contains a machine learning project that detects the presence of Parkinson's Disease in individuals using biomedical voice measurements. By analyzing acoustic features extracted from voice recordings, this model can classify whether a patient has Parkinson's disease or is healthy.

📋 Project Overview

Parkinson's disease is a progressive nervous system disorder that affects movement. One of the early signs of the disease is a change in vocal characteristics. This project utilizes the XGBoost (Extreme Gradient Boosting) algorithm to build a highly accurate classification model based on a dataset of vocal features.

🗄️ Dataset

The dataset used in this project (parkinsons_disease_data.csv) consists of a range of biomedical voice measurements from multiple individuals, some of whom have Parkinson's disease.

Features: Includes various acoustic measurements like fundamental frequency (MDVP:Fo, MDVP:Fhi, MDVP:Flo), jitter, shimmer, and NHR (noise-to-harmonic ratio). The patient name column is excluded during training as it is a non-predictive identifier.

Target (status):

1 - Parkinson's Disease positive

0 - Healthy

🛠️ Technologies & Libraries

This project is built using Python and relies on the following key libraries:

NumPy: For efficient numerical computations and array manipulations.

Pandas: For data loading, manipulation, and analysis.

Scikit-Learn:

MinMaxScaler: To normalize the feature data between 0 and 1.

train_test_split: To safely divide the data into training and testing sets.

accuracy_score: To evaluate model performance.

XGBoost: The core gradient boosting library used for building the XGBClassifier model.

🚀 Installation & Setup

To run this project locally, ensure you have Python installed. You can install the required dependencies using pip:

pip install numpy pandas scikit-learn xgboost


(Note: If you are running this inside a Jupyter Notebook and encounter a ModuleNotFoundError for xgboost, run %pip install xgboost in a notebook cell and restart your kernel).

🧠 Model Pipeline

Data Loading: The dataset is loaded using Pandas.

Data Preprocessing:

The feature matrix (X) is separated from the target labels (y).

The name column is dropped to prevent data leakage.

Features are scaled using MinMaxScaler to ensure all numerical inputs are on a uniform scale (between -1 and 1 or 0 and 1).

Data Splitting: The data is split into training (typically 80%) and testing (20%) sets.

Training: An XGBClassifier is trained on the scaled training data.

Evaluation: The model makes predictions on the test set, and performance is evaluated using the accuracy score.
